{"kind":"request","text":"Operator-directed design task: assess and design extracting the JSONata-with-DDL fold layer into a standalone, reusable library (working name jsonata-ddl-core) that tailapp consumes and gitseq could later consume, so the two do not diverge on the security- and determinism-critical logic. Produce a DESIGN NOTE first (notes/, companion to the architecture note); do not implement yet. Context from a two-repo survey: gitseq only implements this pattern as an UNADOPTED ~1.4kLOC prototype under spike/jsonataddl (its production application layer is a different hand-written fold, workroom-fold@15), while tailapp internal/profile is the mature reference and is already a clean leaf (imports only stdlib + jsonata-go/v206 + ncruces/go-sqlite3; grep for generalbusiness-ai in internal/profile/*.go returns nothing). So this is publish-the-good-one-and-let-the-other-consume-later, NOT a symmetric merge; the near-term consumer may be tailapp alone, and that is acceptable because the extraction also fixes real in-repo problems. The note should decide and specify: (1) MODULE BOUNDARY — what moves into the library (the profile compiler, JSONata subset gate, value-model rules, fold-read grammar + default-deny SQLite authorizer, row-change validation) vs what stays host-side (OTLP canonicalization in internal/ingest, the inbox/frontier/gap machinery in internal/projection, the otel_event/otlp_record staging). (2) PARAMETERIZATION — the domain names currently hard-coded into the grammar must become host-supplied dialect config, not constants: the staging event name (otel_event), the source-record name (otlp_record), the envelope scalar-field set (otlpScalarFields in compiler.go), and the folds/*.jsonata + application.sql source layout. (3) COMPOSITE RUNTIME IDENTITY — the single biggest divergence risk. Today RuntimeID is one repo-global hand-maintained string (tailapp-otlp-1.8-json-v1-ddl-jsonata-v206-sqlite-3.53.4@4 in internal/profile/types.go) that has been bumped for changes in OTHER packages (the @3 bump was for internal/query). Replace it with a COMPOSED identity: a library-owned component (jsonata dialect pin + subset + bounds + DDL grammar + SQLite pin) times host-owned components (canonicalization/value-model version, host SQL-surface version), so two consumers cannot fight over one string and a library change cannot be silently absorbed. (4) VALUE-MODEL DEDUP — the numeric/canonical rules (int64-as-decimal-string, bytes_base64, +/-2^53 integer range, finite-float) are currently DUPLICATED in three tailapp places (internal/ingest/canonical.go, internal/profile/evaluate.go, internal/query/query.go); the library should own one copy so this is fixed within tailapp regardless of whether gitseq ever adopts. (5) SHARED CONTRACT + CONFORMANCE — treat gitseq notes/2026-08-26-jsonata-ddl-application-interface.md (written platform-neutral) as the joint spec the library satisfies, and propose a conformance corpus both consumers can run (tailapp has NO golden fixtures today). (6) MIGRATION SHAPE for tailapp: package move + parameterization is expected to be mostly mechanical since internal/profile is already a leaf, but the note must sequence it against the RuntimeID change (identity composition is a behavior change and needs a RuntimeID bump/versioning story). Two OPEN QUESTIONS the note must pose to the operator, not silently decide: is gitseq actually going to promote its spike to production (this determines two-consumer library vs tailapp-only refactor-with-a-seam), and where does the library live (second module in the tailapp repo, its own repo, or a shared repo). Follow the usual flow: draft the note, publish an exact-head artifact, request checker review; implementation is a later, separate task gated on the note being approved.","body":{"conditions":"A design note exists under notes/ that specifies the jsonata-ddl-core extraction: the module boundary (what moves vs stays host-side), the dialect parameterization removing hard-coded otel_event/otlp_record/otlpScalarFields/source-layout constants, a COMPOSED runtime identity replacing the single repo-global RuntimeID string, consolidation of the triplicated value-model rules into one library copy, adoption of the gitseq platform-neutral interface note as the shared contract plus a proposed conformance corpus, and a tailapp migration sequence tied to a RuntimeID versioning story; the note poses the two open questions (will gitseq promote its spike; where the library lives) to the operator rather than deciding them; builder publishes an exact-head artifact for the note and files a review request to checker, and a checker review verdict is filed on that head. Implementation is explicitly out of scope for this request.","to":"00721374a520eabbbd4ded64dd018110f47545ff20112bee83c75f478e15f818"}}